sections.s2_technical:
Let me build this mapping the way I would when designing a new feature, decision by decision.

The first decision is which side owns the relationship. The foreign key is one column on one table: the `orders` table has a `user_id` column, and there is no column on `users` pointing at orders. So the `Order` is the owning side and carries the foreign-key mapping.

```java
@Entity
public class Order {
    @Id private String orderId;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)   // override default EAGER
    @JoinColumn(name = "user_id")        // the actual FK column on the orders table
    private User user;
}
```

The `User` is the inverse side. `mappedBy = "user"` points at the `user` FIELD on `Order`, telling Hibernate "I do not own this; read the foreign key from the order's user field."

```java
@Entity
public class User {
    @Id private String userId;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "user", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    private List<Order> orders = new ArrayList<>();
}
```

The second decision is cascade and orphan removal. A `User` and their `Order`s form an aggregate here: an order only exists in the context of its user. So `cascade = CascadeType.ALL` means persisting, merging, or removing the user flows to its orders, and `orphanRemoval = true` means an order removed from the collection is deleted. I would NOT use these on a relationship to shared reference data, but for owned children it is exactly right.

The third decision is fetch type. `@ManyToOne` defaults to EAGER, which I always override to LAZY so loading an order does not automatically pull the whole user. `@OneToMany` already defaults to LAZY, which is what I want. When I actually need the user with their orders, I fetch explicitly with a JOIN FETCH query rather than relying on eager loading, so I control exactly one query.

```java
@Query("SELECT u FROM User u LEFT JOIN FETCH u.orders WHERE u.userId = :id")
Optional<User> findWithOrders(@Param("id") String id);
```

The fourth decision is keeping both sides in sync in Java. Because only the owning side drives the foreign-key SQL, adding an order only to the user's collection leaves `user_id` null. I prevent that with helper methods that update both sides atomically.

```java
public void addOrder(Order order) {
    orders.add(order);     // inverse side — in-memory navigation
    order.setUser(this);   // OWNING side — what Hibernate actually persists
}
public void removeOrder(Order order) {
    orders.remove(order);
    order.setUser(null);   // orphanRemoval → DELETE on flush
}
```

A few senior nuances. First, I return DTOs from the service rather than the entities, so lazy associations never leak to the JSON serializer. Second, for the collection I prefer a `List` with an `@OrderColumn` or a `Set` with a business-key `equals`/`hashCode`, to avoid duplicate-handling surprises. Third, I keep the transaction boundary on the service method so the orders load inside an open session. Fourth, this maps one-to-many to a dependent child; if orders could belong to multiple users (they cannot here) it would be a many-to-many with a join table instead.

